libebur128 v1.2.6
Upstream: https://github.com/jiixyj/libebur128
Commit: 67b33abe1558160ed76ada1322329b0e9e058b02

Files: ebur128.c, ebur128.h, COPYING and ebur128/queue/sys/queue.h.
Local adaptation: include the bundled "queue.h" instead of <sys/queue.h>.
The MIT license is in COPYING; the BSD queue license is retained in queue.h.
Compiled through ../../src/loudness.c; no system library or runtime dependency.

Optimized double-input kernels in ../../src/loudness_fast.h use mechanisms from
R128Net 1.1.2, commit 9cc4d4efa05296d3c03bec7ff1e1b218936664e9:
https://github.com/routersys/R128Net
See R128Net-LICENSE.txt for its MIT copyright and permission notice.
The vendor source dispatches double input to these kernels; define
WG_EBUR_REFERENCE to select scalar kernels for verification. Both scalar and optimized kernels
use the local cascaded-biquad K-weighting adaptation.
FMA contraction is disabled in both paths for expression-order comparisons.

Other local adaptations: replace an x86-specific manual-FTZ warning with a
comment; mark the unused interpolator error accumulator as read; compile the
original double filter only in reference builds (other formats are retained).
Stereo gating-block energy additionally uses the paired SIMD helper while
preserving each channel's original addition order, including ring wrap.
Define WG_EBUR_EXTRA_REFERENCE to disable this extra energy optimization and
the WaveGain adapter's peak-only filter bypass for incremental comparisons.

K-weighting retains the shelf and high-pass as two biquads to reduce high-rate
cancellation; state now contains eight values per channel. Integration uses
rounded 400 ms windows and rational 100 ms hops, including ring-boundary splits.
The WaveGain extended peak adapter adds its own screened finite FIR; vendor
true-peak kernels themselves retain their existing coefficients and layout.
